          "title": "1.3 Units, Dimensions, and Measurements",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/6209870/pexels-photo-6209870.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bNaRUh8T_wQ",
          "content": "Physics is a quantitative science, relying heavily on precise measurements of physical quantities. Every measurement consists of a numerical value and a unit.\n\nPhysical Quantity: A property of an object or phenomenon that can be measured (e.g., length, mass, time, force, temperature).\n\nUnit: A standard measure used to express the magnitude of a physical quantity.\n\nInternational System of Units (SI Units):\nThe most widely used system of units in science and engineering. It is based on seven base units from which all other units (derived units) can be formed.\n\nSI Base Quantities and Units:\n\nLength: Meter (m)\n\nMass: Kilogram (kg)\n\nTime: Second (s)\n\nElectric Current: Ampere (A)\n\nThermodynamic Temperature: Kelvin (K)\n\nAmount of Substance: Mole (mol)\n\nLuminous Intensity: Candela (cd)\n\nDerived Units: Combinations of base units.\n\nExamples:\n\nArea: m²\n\nVolume: m³\n\nSpeed: m/s\n\nAcceleration: m/s²\n\nForce: kg⋅m/s² (called a Newton, N)\n\nEnergy: kg⋅m²/s² (called a Joule, J)\n\nPower: J/s (called a Watt, W)\n\nMetric Prefixes:\nUsed to denote multiples or submultiples of SI units, making it convenient to express very large or very small quantities.\n| Prefix | Symbol | Factor | Example |\n| :----- | :----- | :-------- | :------------- |\n| Tera | T | 10¹² | Terameter (Tm) |\n| Giga | G | 10⁹ | Gigawatt (GW) |\n| Mega | M | 10⁶ | Megahertz (MHz)|\n| Kilo | k | 10³ | Kilometer (km) |\n| Hecto | h | 10² | Hectoliter (hL)|\n| Deka | da | 10¹ | Dekagram (dag) |\n| (none) | | 10⁰ | Meter (m) |\n| Deci | d | 10⁻¹ | Decimeter (dm) |\n| Centi | c | 10⁻² | Centimeter (cm)|\n| Milli | m | 10⁻³ | Milligram (mg) |\n| Micro | µ | 10⁻⁶ | Micrometer (µm)|\n| Nano | n | 10⁻⁹ | Nanosecond (ns)|\n| Pico | p | 10⁻¹² | Picofarad (pF) |\n| Femto | f | 10⁻¹⁵ | Femtometer (fm)|\n\nUnit Conversion:\nThe ability to convert between different units (e.g., SI to other systems like British Imperial, or between prefixed units) is essential. This is often done using conversion factors.\n\nDimensions:\nThe dimension of a physical quantity refers to the fundamental physical nature of that quantity, independent of the specific units used to measure it. Dimensions are usually expressed in terms of the base quantities (Length [L], Mass [M], Time [T], etc.).\n\nExamples:\n\nDimension of length = [L]\n\nDimension of area ([L] x [L]) = [L²]\n\nDimension of speed ([L] / [T]) = [LT⁻¹]\n\nDimension of force ([M][L][T⁻²])\n\nDimensional Analysis:\nA powerful tool used to:\n\nCheck the consistency of equations: Both sides of a valid physical equation must have the same dimensions. If they don't, the equation is incorrect.\n\nDerive relationships between physical quantities: Can help in deducing the form of an equation, although it doesn't determine numerical constants.\n\nConvert units.\n\nMeasurements:\nThe process of determining the value of a physical quantity by comparison with a standard.\n\nAll measurements have some degree of uncertainty due to limitations of measuring instruments, experimental procedures, and human error.\n\nIt is crucial to estimate and report the uncertainty associated with any measurement.",
