          "title": "10.4 Continuity Equation and Bernoulli’s Principle (Fluid Dynamics)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/30945518/pexels-photo-30945518.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KHr0xy006nM",
          "content": "Assumptions for Ideal Fluid Flow (often made for simplification):\n\nSteady (Laminar) Flow: Velocity of the fluid at any point is constant over time (no turbulence). Streamlines (paths of fluid particles) do not cross.\n\nIncompressible Flow: Density of the fluid is constant. (Good approximation for liquids, less so for gases at high speeds).\n\nNon-viscous Flow: No internal friction (viscosity) in the fluid. (Viscosity causes resistance to flow).\n\nIrrotational Flow: Fluid elements do not rotate about their center of mass.\n\nEquation of Continuity (Conservation of Mass for Fluids):\nFor an incompressible fluid flowing through a pipe of varying cross-sectional area:\nA₁v₁ = A₂v₂\nwhere A₁ and A₂ are the cross-sectional areas at two points, and v₁ and v₂ are the corresponding fluid speeds at those points.\n\nThe product Av is the volume flow rate (volume of fluid passing a point per unit time), which is constant.\n\nThis means that where the pipe narrows (A decreases), the fluid speed v must increase, and vice versa.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A diagram of fluid flowing through a pipe with varying cross-sectional area, illustrating the continuity equation.)\n\nBernoulli's Principle (Conservation of Energy for Fluids):\nRelates pressure, speed, and height for an ideal fluid in steady flow. Derived from the work-energy theorem applied to a fluid element.\nBernoulli's Equation: P₁ + ½ρv₁² + ρgy₁ = P₂ + ½ρv₂² + ρgy₂ = constant\nwhere:\n\nP is the absolute pressure in the fluid.\n\nρ is the density of the fluid.\n\nv is the speed of the fluid.\n\ng is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\ny is the elevation (height) above a reference level.\nEach term has units of pressure (or energy per unit volume).\n\nP: Static pressure.\n\n½ρv²: Dynamic pressure (related to kinetic energy per unit volume).\n\nρgy: Hydrostatic pressure (related to potential energy per unit volume).\n\nConsequences and Applications of Bernoulli's Principle:\n\nWhere speed is higher, pressure is lower (if height is constant):\n\nLift on an airplane wing (air flows faster over the curved top surface, lower pressure above wing).\n\nVenturi meter (measures flow speed by pressure difference in constricted section).\n\nPerfume atomizer, carburetor.\n\nCurveball in baseball.\n\nFluid flowing out of a hole in a tank (Torricelli's Theorem): v = sqrt(2gh).\n\n(Suggested insertion: Diagrams illustrating Bernoulli's principle: flow through a Venturi tube showing changes in speed and pressure. Lift on an airfoil.)\n\nViscosity (Real Fluids):\nReal fluids have internal friction (viscosity, η), which resists flow. Viscosity leads to energy losses. This is not covered by the ideal fluid model but is important in many real-world applications (e.g., Poiseuille's Law for viscous flow in pipes, Stokes' Law for drag on a sphere in a viscous fluid).",
