          "title": "15.4 Polarization",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/6209870/pexels-photo-6209870.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KwE1Db82PEk",
          "content": "Light is a transverse electromagnetic wave, meaning the electric field (E-field) and magnetic field (B-field) oscillate perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation.\n*   **Unpolarized Light:** The E-field vectors oscillate in random directions in the plane perpendicular to propagation (e.g., light from the sun, an incandescent bulb).\n*   **Polarized Light:** The E-field vectors oscillate in a single plane (plane-polarized or linearly polarized light), or in a specific rotating pattern (circularly or elliptically polarized).\n\n**Methods of Polarization:**\n1.  **Polarization by Selective Absorption (using Polarizing Filters):**\n    *   A polarizing filter (e.g., Polaroid sheet) contains long-chain molecules aligned in a specific direction. It selectively transmits light waves whose E-field vectors are aligned parallel to its transmission axis and absorbs waves whose E-field vectors are perpendicular to it.\n    *   **Malus's Law:** If plane-polarized light of intensity `I₀` is incident on a polarizer (analyzer) whose transmission axis makes an angle `θ` with the direction of polarization, the transmitted intensity `I` is:\n        `I = I₀ cos²(θ)`\n    *   When unpolarized light passes through a polarizer, the transmitted intensity is `I₀/2`.\n2.  **Polarization by Reflection:**\n    *   When unpolarized light reflects off a nonmetallic surface (e.g., water, glass), the reflected light is partially or completely polarized, with the E-field vector preferentially parallel to the reflecting surface.\n    *   **Brewster's Angle (θ_B) (Polarizing Angle):** The angle of incidence at which the reflected light is completely polarized (E-field parallel to surface). The reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other at Brewster's angle.\n        `tan(θ_B) = n₂ / n₁` (where light is incident from medium `n₁` onto `n₂`).\n3.  **Polarization by Scattering:**\n    *   When light is scattered by small particles (e.g., air molecules), the scattered light is partially polarized. Light scattered at 90° to the incident direction is most strongly polarized. (Reason why the sky appears polarized).\n\n*(Suggested insertion: Diagrams illustrating unpolarized light vs. plane-polarized light (showing E-field oscillations). Diagram of a polarizing filter and Malus's law setup. Diagram of polarization by reflection at Brewster's angle.)*\n\n**Applications of Polarization:**\n*   Polaroid sunglasses (reduce glare from horizontal surfaces).\n*   LCD screens (liquid crystal displays use polarization).\n*   Photography (polarizing filters to reduce glare, enhance sky color).\n*   Scientific instruments (e.g., polarimeters to study optically active substances).\n*   3D movie technology.",
