          "title": "17.4 RC Circuits and Capacitors",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/8471851/pexels-photo-8471851.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTTZLj1DQnc",
          "content": "An **RC circuit** contains a resistor (R) and a capacitor (C) connected to a voltage source (e.g., a battery with an EMF ε) and a switch. Capacitors store charge, and their behavior in circuits is time-dependent.\n\n**Charging a Capacitor:**\nWhen a switch is closed, connecting an uncharged capacitor in series with a resistor and a battery:\n*   Current `I` begins to flow, and charge `Q` accumulates on the capacitor plates.\n*   The voltage across the capacitor `ΔV_C = Q/C` increases as it charges.\n*   According to KVL: `ε - IR - Q/C = 0`. Since `I = dQ/dt`, this is a differential equation.\n*   **Charge `Q(t)` on the capacitor as a function of time `t`:**\n    `Q(t) = Q_final (1 - e^(-t/τ)) = Cε (1 - e^(-t/τ))`\n*   **Current `I(t)` in the circuit as a function of time `t`:**\n    `I(t) = (dQ/dt) = (ε/R) e^(-t/τ) = I_initial e^(-t/τ)`\n*   **Voltage across capacitor `ΔV_C(t)`:** `ΔV_C(t) = Q(t)/C = ε (1 - e^(-t/τ))`\n*   **Time Constant (τ, tau):** `τ = RC`. A measure of how quickly the capacitor charges.\n    *   After one time constant (`t = τ`), the capacitor charges to `~63.2%` of its final charge (`Q_final = Cε`).\n    *   Current drops to `~36.8%` of its initial value.\n*   Initially (`t=0`), capacitor acts like a short circuit (`ΔV_C = 0, I = ε/R`).\n*   After a long time (`t → ∞`), capacitor acts like an open circuit (`ΔV_C = ε, I = 0`, fully charged).\n\n**Discharging a Capacitor:**\nWhen a charged capacitor (`Q₀` initial charge) is connected across a resistor (with the battery removed):\n*   The capacitor discharges, and current flows through the resistor.\n*   **Charge `Q(t)` on the capacitor:**\n    `Q(t) = Q₀ e^(-t/τ)`\n*   **Current `I(t)` in the circuit:**\n    `I(t) = -(dQ/dt) = (Q₀/RC) e^(-t/τ) = I₀ e^(-t/τ)` (Magnitude decreases exponentially).\n*   **Voltage across capacitor `ΔV_C(t)`:** `ΔV_C(t) = Q(t)/C = (Q₀/C) e^(-t/τ) = ΔV₀ e^(-t/τ)`\n*   Time constant `τ = RC` is also characteristic for discharge.\n\n*(Suggested insertion: Circuit diagrams for charging an RC circuit (with battery and switch) and discharging an RC circuit. Graphs of Q(t), I(t), and ΔV_C(t) vs. time for both charging and discharging, showing exponential behavior and the role of the time constant τ.)*",
